51 World Championships changed that perception of events. Despite local opposition in Alpe d’Huez, Germany was readmitted to that year’s championships, and the heavyweight crew piloted by " [:a {:href "/athletes/96104"} "Anderl Ostler"] " walked away with the gold medals."] "\n\n" [:p {} "During training runs on the Olympic course, both German teams surprisingly struggled and looked set to miss the medal positions. The decision was made to withdrew one of their teams and enter a composite crew made of the " [:a {:href "/results/281"} "two man"] " champions Ostler and " [:a {:href "/athletes/95820"} "Lorenz Nieberl"] ", together with the two heaviest members of the other German foursome. This proved to be a winning combination and the crew posted the fastest time of all four runs to win the championships. Ostler became the first man to do the two-man/four-man double at an Olympic Games. A film, " [:em {} "Schwere Jungs"] ", based very loosely on this story, was released in Germany in 2007."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In third place was the team piloted by multiple World champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/85659"} "Fritz Feierabend"] ", who won his fifth Olympic medal but was never to become Olympic champion."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Fifteen (15) teams contested the event, after the withdrawal of the Belgian team following a practice accident."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {} "Run #1"] [:th {} "Run #2"] [:th {} "Run #3"] [:th {} "Run #4"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} "Unified Team of Germany"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} "5:07.84"] [:td {} "1:16.86 (1)"] [:td {} "1:17.57 (1)"] [:td {} "1:16.55 (1)"] [:td {} "1:16.86 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}]